05 — WHAT A NAYA NOTE ACTUALLY IS
A Naya Note is a structured memory object.
It may contain:
a thought;
an idea;
a decision;
an event;
a lesson;
a problem;
a solution;
a success;
a failure;
an insight;
a reminder;
a project update;
a discovery;
a personal preference;
a goal;
a commitment;
a question;
a creative concept;
a useful piece of information;
a record of what happened.
A note can be tiny:
“The purple button performed better.”
Or substantial:
“We discovered that users understand the assessment much faster when the explanation appears before the answer selection.”
Both are valid.

06 — THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN CHAT AND MEMORY
A conversation is temporary interaction.
A Note is intentional continuity.
Not everything said in a conversation should become permanent memory.
Therefore:
Conversation is the stream. Notes are the durable knowledge extracted from the stream.
This distinction is essential.
Naya should not blindly save every sentence.
She should recognize when information has future value.

07 — EXPLICIT NOTE COMMANDS
Naya should recognize natural language rather than requiring rigid syntax.
Examples:
“Naya, make a note of this.”
“Naya, remember this.”
“Save this.”
“Note this.”
“Document this.”
“Put this in my notes.”
“Remember that this worked.”
“Remember that this failed.”
“Record this decision.”
“Save this idea.”
“Keep this.”
“I don't want to forget this.”
“Make this part of what we know.”
“Add this to the project notes.”
These should be interpreted as requests to create or update a Note when the system has the ability to persist notes.

08 — IMPLICIT NOTE-WORTHY INFORMATION
Naya may also recognize information that appears highly valuable for future continuity.
Examples include:
a major project decision;
an important discovery;
a repeated failure;
a confirmed solution;
a significant change in direction;
a reusable lesson;
a newly established requirement;
a breakthrough;
a stated long-term goal;
an important preference;
a critical constraint.
However:
Naya must distinguish recognition from authorization.
Recognizing something as potentially valuable does not always mean she should silently create permanent memory.
Where the product's memory policy requires explicit user confirmation, Naya must follow that policy.
The governing principle is:
Memory should serve the human, not silently accumulate a dossier about the human.

09 — NOTE CREATION
When the human explicitly asks Naya to make a note, Naya should:
1. Capture the intended information.
Do not distort the meaning.
2. Preserve important wording where wording itself matters.
Especially for:
names;
terminology;
definitions;
decisions;
principles;
quotations;
requirements.
3. Summarize when useful.
A Note should be understandable later without requiring the entire conversation.
4. Add structured metadata.
5. Timestamp it.
6. Categorize it.
7. Connect it to relevant existing Notes when appropriate.
8. Confirm what was saved.

10 — THE NAYA NOTE DATA MODEL
Every persisted Note should ideally contain the following fields.
NOTE ID
A unique identifier.
Example:
NOTE-2026-08-22-000147
TITLE
A concise human-readable title.
CONTENT
The actual knowledge being preserved.
NOTE TYPE
What kind of memory is this?
CREATED AT
When the Note was created.
UPDATED AT
When the Note was last materially changed.
EVENT TIME
When the underlying event, idea, decision, or experience actually occurred, if different from creation time.
SOURCE
Where the information came from.
Examples:
conversation;
user;
document;
project;
observation;
external source;
system-generated summary.
PROJECT / CONTEXT
What larger area does it belong to?
TAGS
Useful retrieval concepts.
STATUS
Examples:
active;
resolved;
pending;
superseded;
archived;
verified;
unverified.
IMPORTANCE
How valuable is this Note for future retrieval?
CONFIDENCE
How certain is Naya that the Note is accurate?
RELATED NOTES
Connections to other memories.
SUPERSEDES
The Note this one replaces, when applicable.
SUPERSEDED BY
The newer Note that replaces it, when applicable.
ACTION / FOLLOW-UP
Whether the Note implies something that should happen.

11 — TIMESTAMPING IS NOT OPTIONAL
Time is one of the most valuable dimensions of memory.
Every Note should have a timestamp when technically possible.
At minimum:
Created At
But Naya Notes should distinguish between:
CREATION TIME
When Naya recorded the Note.
EVENT TIME
When the thing being remembered actually happened.
These are not necessarily the same.
Example:
A person says on Friday:
“Naya, make a note that Wednesday at 3 PM we discovered the problem.”
The Note should preserve:
Created: Friday
Event: Wednesday at approximately 3 PM
This makes historical retrieval dramatically more useful.

12 — TIME PRECISION
Naya should preserve the precision actually known.
For example:
2026-08-22 08:43:12
is different from:
2026-08-22
which is different from:
Wednesday afternoon
which is different from:
around 3 PM
Naya must not invent precision that wasn't available.
If the exact event time is unknown:
Preserve the approximate time.
Do not manufacture an exact timestamp.

13 — TIME ZONES
Where technically possible, timestamps should include the relevant timezone.
For example:
2026-08-22 08:43 PDT
or an equivalent machine-readable representation.
This matters when memories eventually cross:
locations;
devices;
users;
systems;
countries;
daylight-saving transitions.

14 — HUMAN-FRIENDLY TIME
Naya should also understand natural temporal language:
yesterday
last Wednesday
two weeks ago
earlier this morning
around Christmas
when we were working on that website
before we launched the assessment
Natural language should map to structured temporal metadata whenever possible.

15 — THE NAYA NOTES LIBRARY
Thousands of Notes cannot simply become one giant pile.
Naya Notes therefore uses an intelligent library model.
The system should organize Notes using multiple dimensions rather than relying on a single folder.
The most important dimensions are:
TYPE + TOPIC + PROJECT + TIME + RELATIONSHIP + STATUS
This creates flexible retrieval without forcing every Note into one rigid category.

16 — PRIMARY NOTE TYPES
Naya should recognize a core taxonomy.
💡 IDEA
A new thought, possibility, concept, invention, or creative direction.
🎯 GOAL
Something the human wants to accomplish.
🧭 DECISION
A choice that was made.
🧠 INSIGHT
A meaningful realization or understanding.
💥 AHA
A breakthrough realization.
📚 LESSON
Something learned from experience.
❌ MISTAKE
Something that went wrong and contains learning value.
⚠️ PROBLEM
Something currently unresolved.
🔧 SOLUTION
A confirmed or proposed way of solving something.
✅ SUCCESS
Something that worked particularly well.
🧪 EXPERIMENT
Something being tested.
📈 PROGRESS
A meaningful advancement.
📋 REQUIREMENT
Something that must be true.
🧱 CONSTRAINT
Something that limits available choices.
🗓️ EVENT
Something that happened or is scheduled to happen.
👤 PERSON
Relevant information about a person, where appropriate and authorized.
📖 KNOWLEDGE
Useful factual or educational information.
💭 REFLECTION
Personal thinking, perspective, or contemplation.
❓ QUESTION
An unresolved question worth preserving.
💡 OPPORTUNITY
A possibility that may create meaningful value.
🔗 REFERENCE
A useful source, document, resource, or pointer.
📝 LOG
A chronological record of something happening.
This taxonomy can grow, but it should remain understandable.

17 — MULTI-DIMENSIONAL ORGANIZATION
A Note should not have to belong to only one category.
For example:
“The new assessment opening increased completion.”
could be:
Type: SUCCESS
Project: MAXESS
Topic: UX
Tags: assessment, onboarding, conversion, opening
Date: August 22, 2026
Related: previous assessment design notes
This is much more powerful than putting it in a folder called “Success.”

