This allows queries such as:
“Show me everything we've learned about MAXESS.”
or:
“What decisions have we made about Naya?”

20 — RELATIONSHIP ORGANIZATION
This is where Naya Notes becomes significantly more powerful.
Notes should be capable of connecting to other Notes.
For example:
Problem
↓
Experiment
↓
Solution
↓
Success
↓
Lesson
↓
New Standard
Instead of isolated notes, Naya can build a knowledge graph.

21 — NOTE RELATIONSHIPS
Useful relationship types include:
RELATED TO
General relationship.
CAUSED
One event contributed to another.
RESULTED IN
One thing produced another.
SUPPORTS
One Note provides evidence for another.
CONTRADICTS
One Note conflicts with another.
REFINES
One Note improves the understanding of another.
SUPERSEDES
A newer Note replaces an older one.
DERIVED FROM
A Note was created from another source.
LEARNED FROM
A lesson came from an event, problem, or experiment.
RESOLVES
A solution addresses a problem.
FOLLOWS
Chronological continuation.
This allows memory to become connected knowledge.

22 — THE KNOWLEDGE CHAIN
A particularly important Naya Notes pattern is:
EVENT → OBSERVATION → INSIGHT → LESSON → DECISION → ACTION → RESULT
For example:
EVENT
Users abandoned the assessment at Question 4.
↓
OBSERVATION
Completion dropped after the long instructional screen.
↓
INSIGHT
The instructional interruption may be creating unnecessary friction.
↓
LESSON
Teaching should not interrupt momentum unnecessarily.
↓
DECISION
Move the explanation into the question experience.
↓
ACTION
Redesign the flow.
↓
RESULT
Completion improves.
That entire chain can become reusable intelligence.

23 — SUCCESS NOTES
Naya should deliberately preserve successful patterns.
A success should not merely be:
“It worked.”
Where possible, capture:
What worked?
Why do we believe it worked?
What conditions were present?
What should we repeat?
Where else could this apply?
This turns success into reusable knowledge.

24 — FAILURE NOTES
Failures deserve equal attention.
When something fails, Naya should capture:
What happened?
What was expected?
What actually happened?
Root cause, if known?
What was tried?
What failed?
What fixed it?
What should we avoid next time?
What guardrail should exist?
This prevents the system from repeatedly paying the same tuition.

25 — LESSON NOTES
A Lesson is more valuable than a raw event.
Instead of:
“The button was hard to see.”
A Lesson might be:
“Important action buttons require sufficient contrast and visual prominence; otherwise users may overlook the intended next step.”
The second can be reused across future work.

26 — DECISION NOTES
Important decisions should preserve not only what was decided, but why.
A Decision Note should ideally contain:
DECISION
What was chosen?
REASON
Why?
ALTERNATIVES
What other options were considered?
CONSTRAINTS
What influenced the decision?
EXPECTED OUTCOME
What should happen?
DATE
When was the decision made?
OWNER
Who made or authorized it, where relevant?
This makes future reversal or reconsideration intelligent rather than confusing.

27 — DECISION HISTORY
If a decision changes:
Do not simply overwrite the old Note.
Preserve the historical record.
Create the new state and connect it to the old one.
For example:
Decision A
↓
Superseded by Decision B
This allows Naya to answer:
“Why did we change this?”
rather than merely:
“What are we doing now?”

28 — VERSIONED MEMORY
Important Notes may evolve.
Therefore Naya should distinguish:
Current knowledge
from:
Historical knowledge
A previous belief can remain historically useful without remaining currently authoritative.
This is critical.
History should be preserved without allowing outdated information to masquerade as current truth.

29 — CONFLICTING NOTES
When two Notes conflict, Naya should not silently choose one.
She should examine:
timestamps;
sources;
authority;
verification;
context;
whether one supersedes the other.
Then classify the relationship.
Possible result:
“The earlier Note said X. On August 22, this was updated to Y after verification.”
This is much more trustworthy than silently rewriting history.

30 — NOTE CONFIDENCE
Not every memory is equally reliable.
Naya should support confidence levels such as:
VERIFIED
Strongly established.
HIGH CONFIDENCE
Very likely correct.
MODERATE CONFIDENCE
Reasonably supported.
LOW CONFIDENCE
Tentative.
UNVERIFIED
Not yet confirmed.
USER-STATED
Recorded because the human stated it, without independent verification.
This is especially important for factual or consequential information.

31 — SOURCE PROVENANCE
Naya should preserve where important information came from.
Possible sources:
USER
The human explicitly said it.
CONVERSATION
Derived from a conversation.
DOCUMENT
Taken from a document.
SYSTEM
Generated by system activity.
OBSERVATION
Directly observed.
EXTERNAL SOURCE
Retrieved from an external source.
INFERENCE
Derived through reasoning.
This lets Naya answer:
“Why do you remember that?”
with an actual provenance trail.

32 — NAYA MUST NOT INVENT MEMORY
This is a fundamental law.
Naya must never claim to remember something merely because it seems plausible.
She must not fabricate:
Notes;
timestamps;
conversations;
decisions;
events;
preferences;
previous actions;
stored information;
retrieval results.
If the memory cannot be retrieved:
Say so.
If the system lacks persistent storage:
Say so.
If the information is uncertain:
Say so.
Trust is more important than the appearance of continuity.

33 — THE RETRIEVAL ENGINE
Naya Notes should support multiple retrieval modes.
EXACT
Find the specific phrase or title.
SEMANTIC
Find Notes about the same idea even when wording differs.
TEMPORAL
Find Notes from a date or period.
PROJECT
Find Notes belonging to a project.
TYPE
Find all Decisions, Lessons, Ideas, Problems, etc.
TOPIC
Find Notes about a subject.
RELATIONSHIP
Find Notes connected to another Note.
STATUS
Find unresolved, active, archived, or superseded Notes.
COMBINED
Use multiple dimensions together.
This is essential when the library becomes large.

34 — NATURAL-LANGUAGE RETRIEVAL
The human should not need to know the database structure.
They should be able to ask:
“What did we learn about this?”
“What have I said about this?”
“What ideas did I have last month?”
“What problems are still unresolved?”
“What decisions have we made?”
“What worked?”
“What didn't work?”
“Show me my notes from Wednesday.”
“What were we talking about around 3 PM?”
“What did we decide before launching?”
“What did I say about Naya Notes?”
Naya translates natural language into the appropriate retrieval strategy.

35 — TEMPORAL RETRIEVAL
Time-based recall is a core capability.
Examples:
“What did we talk about last Wednesday?”
“What happened around 3 PM?”
“What did I learn last month?”
“Show me the notes from our last meeting.”
“What was I working on yesterday afternoon?”
“What decisions did I make this week?”
Naya should combine:
time + semantic meaning + project + note type
when useful.

36 — CONVERSATION RECONSTRUCTION
When sufficient metadata and conversation records exist, Naya should be able to reconstruct meaningful historical context.
For example:
“What were we talking about last Wednesday around 3?”
Naya should search the relevant temporal window, identify the relevant conversation or Notes, and summarize the relevant context.
She should distinguish:
What was actually recorded
from:
What Naya is inferring from the available record.

37 — MEMORY SUMMARIZATION
Naya should be able to transform many Notes into a higher-level understanding.
For example:
“Summarize everything we've learned about this project.”
The result should not simply dump 87 Notes.
It should synthesize:
major discoveries;
important decisions;
unresolved problems;
successful patterns;
lessons;
current state;
important historical context;
recommended next considerations.
This is where Notes become intelligence.

38 — MEMORY → BRAIN
Naya Notes feeds Naya Brain.
The relationship is:
NOTES → KNOWLEDGE → CONTEXT → REASONING → ACTION
Notes provide accumulated evidence.
Brain uses relevant Notes to improve future thinking.
But Brain should not retrieve everything.
Retrieve what matters to the current problem.

39 — MEMORY RELEVANCE
A Note's usefulness depends on:
RELEVANCE
Does it relate to the current task?
RECENCY
Is it still current?
AUTHORITY
Is the source trustworthy?
IMPORTANCE
Does it materially affect the outcome?
VERIFICATION
Has it been confirmed?
CONNECTION
Does it connect to other important knowledge?
The best memory is not necessarily the newest memory.
It is the most relevant trustworthy memory.

40 — MEMORY DECAY AND OBSOLESCENCE
Some information becomes outdated.
Examples:
old prices;
old plans;
old technical configurations;
outdated requirements;
previous strategies;
temporary preferences;
old project states.
Naya should not automatically delete historical information.
Instead, where appropriate, mark it:
OUTDATED
SUPERSEDED
ARCHIVED
HISTORICAL
This preserves learning without contaminating current reasoning.

41 — ACTIVE VS HISTORICAL MEMORY
Naya should distinguish:
ACTIVE MEMORY
Currently relevant.
HISTORICAL MEMORY
Useful for understanding what happened.
ARCHIVED MEMORY
Retained but unlikely to affect current work.
SUPERSEDED MEMORY
Replaced by newer information.
This prevents memory from becoming a source of confusion.

42 — MEMORY CONSOLIDATION
When many Notes express the same lesson, Naya should be able to consolidate them.
For example:
50 separate Notes may reveal:
“Users struggle when instructions interrupt action.”
Naya can create a higher-level:
MASTER LESSON
“Preserve user momentum by integrating necessary instruction into the action flow rather than unnecessarily interrupting it.”
The supporting Notes remain available.
This creates:
MANY EXPERIENCES → ONE REUSABLE PRINCIPLE

